ome games, okay? Um, and these games are obviously to help with adding, subtracting. We may try multiplication and division today. Maybe. I found a game that does that. We'll give it a try.\nTurn 26. TUTOR: Um, yeah. Do you have any questions or like, do you want me to review something with you next time? Do you have any suggestions?\nTurn 27. STUDENT: No.\nTurn 28. TUTOR: No. Yeah, if you ever come up with something like, maybe, hey, I'm confused about fractions, negative numbers, you know, all that stuff, just let me know, okay? And we can talk about it instead of playing games, okay?\nTurn 29. STUDENT: Okay.\nTurn 30. TUTOR: Um, but maybe I'll try and find games or activities that cover those as well. I mean, we do have a learning platform, right? We have a learning platform, but I feel like a learning platform is a little bit too serious for summer. So, let me see which topic we're even on with a learning platform. Um, yeah, we haven't used a learning platform in so long. Maybe we will next week.\nTurn 31. TUTOR: So, this is the plan. We're gonna start with the usual, well, we're gonna start with the quick math game. And I'll try and do that often. Because the more you see the math, the more you, um, you start to understand it better, right? And then get quicker at it.\nTurn 32. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The tutor's screen changes to a black loading screen with a blue spinning circle.\nTurn 32. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p1 - 'QUICK MATH!' (start)] The game \"QUICK MATH!\" loads on a teal background. It shows a cartoon character, a \"BEST 25\" score, and three buttons: a question mark, a play button, and a Twitter logo.\nClicks the play button.\nTurn 32. TUTOR: So this game is, we played it the first game we played yesterday. Basically, you just click the correct one or tell me the correct one and I'll click it. And it's, it's mostly adding and subtracting. Okay?\nTurn 33. STUDENT: Okay.\nTurn 34. TUTOR: All right. Let's get started. You ready?\nTurn 35. STUDENT: Yeah