_CHANGE: p7 - '9 - 6 = 3', '2 + 4 = -1', '1 - 2 = -10' (start)] The next set of problems appears.\nTutor clicks the top option, '9 - 6 = 3'.\nTurn 48. STUDENT: The top one.\nTurn 49. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p8 - '6 + 6 = 16', '0 + 3 = 3', '9 - 6 = 4' (start)] The next set of problems appears. The score is 11.\nTutor clicks the middle option, '0 + 3 = 3'.\nTurn 49. STUDENT: The middle one.\nTurn 50. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p9 - '0 + 0 = 6', '0 - 7 = 1', '4 + 3 = 7', '6 + 2 = 0' (start)] The next set of problems appears. The score is 14.\nTurn 50. STUDENT: The th-\nTurn 51. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The screen changes to \"Too Slow!!!\" with a score of 14.\nTurn 51. TUTOR: Good. All right, let's try again. You did very good.\nTurn 52. STUDENT: Okay.\n\nRespond as the TUTOR. Give only your response, no labels or prefixes.\n\nNEXT REPLY INSTRUCTION:\nProvide targeted scaffolding now. Identify the specific unresolved obstacle in the student's current mathematical work. Offer only the smallest useful hint, representation, or clarification that makes the next step accessible. Use the learner's actual numbers and ideas when available. Leave the key calculation or inference for the student and invite one manageable next step. Do not give a full solution, stack several leading questions, repeat an explanation the learner already understands, or invent unseen worksheet details.\nFor this next reply only, use the specified policy in context. Give the student one clear, manageable invitation to think. Be mathematically accurate and concise. Do not mention policy names, routing, evaluation, or these instructions.\n/no_think"
